{"kind":"artifact","text":"notes/2026-10-01-jam-room.md at exact 0861580ea3b3d3c50a3f9a055d5614daf1cbfa17 on request/jam-room-r6, under promise git:sha1…5219771 on request git:sha1…3969c35\n\nJam room, revision 8 at 0861580ea3b3d3c50a3f9a055d5614daf1cbfa17 on request/jam-room-r6, for request 9ce8533f, answering the checker's changes-requested verdict 6a6a2f7a on 798ac2a3. Subject: notes/2026-10-01-jam-room.md. Design only; nothing was run; all bar arithmetic is worked by hand and labelled so. The one finding is repaired by one rule that keeps the kept design: a configuration value is recorded at its entry's time and active from its own effect bar; an entry's effect bar, for a pattern, an interpretation or a rules change alike, is computed with the configuration that is active at that entry's time, and with no pending change. In the verdict's example the pattern at 10.5 uses six seconds and lands on boundary 18. The reviewer should look first at one clause that this revision adds to the kept rule: an entry's effect bar is never before the effect bar of the entry before it in sequence. It only ever delays an entry. It was chosen over a per-instrument form because it also keeps tempo changes and rules changes in entry order, so no separate tie rule is needed; the note marks it as the author's choice that the planner may replace. Consequences are stated with worked examples: after a decrease, an entry recorded just after activation is held to the bar of the entry before it; an increase reorders nothing and skips some bars; assigned bars never change; of two pending changes the second uses the value from before both, and they become active in entry order; a pattern that names a pending interpretation does not start before it. The record needs no active flag: the founding value and each rules act's entry with its sequence and time are enough. Two claims are hand reasoning that the note asks the eventual fixture to check: that the added clause matters only after a decrease, and that an active change stays active across a later tempo change. Gap P9 is restated: the jam's rules item is application data under the jam's own definition and is not the repository's rules scope; what it needs from the platform is a hosted room founded under such a definition. From the scopes guide the author confirmed that a value slot may be not fixed and that a value effect writes it from a field; not confirmed: an example of such an act, how the item gets its founding value, and that the jam's definition validates, since it is not written.","body":{"commit":"0861580ea3b3d3c50a3f9a055d5614daf1cbfa17","path":"notes/2026-10-01-jam-room.md"}}